<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woody Guthrie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Woodrow Wilson Guthrie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woody Guthrie was an American singer, songwriter and composer of folk music whose work dealt with American socialism and anti-fascism; his guitar often carried the words &quot;This machine kills fascists&quot;. Raised in Okemah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, he joined the migrants who left the Dust Bowl for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> radio before moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where in 1940 he recorded the album Dust Bowl Ballads and wrote &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/this-land-is-your-land">This Land Is Your Land</a>&quot; in answer to Irving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s &quot;God Bless America&quot;. He sang with the Almanac Singers, joined the Merchant Marine in 1943 and wrote the autobiography Bound for Glory. Huntington's disease, diagnosed in 1952, kept him in hospitals from 1956 until his death in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie.jpg" alt="Woody Guthrie" width="330" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Al Aumuller/ New York World-Telegram and the Sun (uploaded by Urban) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woody_Guthrie_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its theme may come from a passage in <b>Woody Guthrie</b>'s autobiography Bound for Glory (1943), whose work had shaped Dylan's political thinking.</blockquote><a href="/songs/blowin-in-the-wind">Blowin' in the Wind</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Jarama Valley&quot;, a song about the Battle of Jarama in the Spanish Civil War, used its tune and was recorded by <b>Woody Guthrie</b> and the Almanac Singers with Pete Seeger.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote>Harry McClintock is credited as the first to sing it in public, and <b>Woody Guthrie</b>, Pete Seeger and Utah Phillips sang it later.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-preacher-and-the-slave">The Preacher and the Slave</a></li><li><blockquote>The American folk singer <b>Woody Guthrie</b> wrote &quot;This Land Is Your Land&quot; on 23 February 1940, in his room at the Hanover House hotel on 43rd Street in New York.</blockquote><a href="/songs/this-land-is-your-land">This Land Is Your Land</a></li></ul></section>
